.TH FUSE.MIRRORFS 8 "November 2026" "mirrorfs 0.1.0" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
fuse.mirrorfs \- mount mirrorfs from /etc/fstab
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY mount
.B \-t fuse.mirrorfs
.I local mountpoint
.B \-o
.I remote=path\fR[\fI,option\fR...]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
An
.B /etc/fstab
entry of type
.B fuse.mirrorfs
is mounted by
.BR mount.fuse3 (8).
That helper executes the
.B mirrorfs
program, which the Debian package installs at
.IR /usr/bin/mirrorfs .
The boot
.B PATH
includes
.IR /usr/bin ,
so the entry does not need a full path to the binary.
.PP
.B mount.fuse3
calls
.B mirrorfs
with the local directory, the mount point, and a single
.B \-o
list.
Without the
.B foreground
option,
.B mirrorfs
forks.
The parent exits 0 after the mount is ready, and the child keeps serving.
Putting
.B foreground
in fstab makes
.BR mount (8)
wait until the filesystem is unmounted, which stalls boot.
.PP
The local directory is the only source of truth.
Mutations are applied there first and then replayed onto
.IR remote= .
Reads never fall back to the remote directory.
See
.BR mirrorfs (1)
for the foreground command and the queue.
.SH FSTAB
.EX
/mnt/claude_local  /mnt/claude  fuse.mirrorfs  remote=/mnt/claude_remote,remote-fs=nfs,remote-check=5m,allow_other,nodev,nosuid,_netdev,nofail,x-systemd.requires-mounts-for=/mnt/claude_local  0  0
.EE
.PP
The first field is the local directory.
.I remote=
is required and is the directory that receives the mirror.
That directory can be the mount root or a folder inside the mount.
The fifth and sixth fields are dump and pass, both 0.
.PP
.B remote-fs=nfs
keeps mirrorfs mounted when that path is not NFS yet.
Mutations stay queued until a check thread sees
.B nfs
or
.BR nfs4 .
Do not put
.BI x-systemd.requires-mounts-for=
on the remote path: systemd would then refuse to start mirrorfs after the
NFS mount failed.
Keep that requirement for the local directory.
.B _netdev
orders this mount after the network.
.B nofail
lets boot continue when
.B mirrorfs
fails.
.B nodev
and
.B nosuid
are forwarded to the kernel.
A path that contains a comma cannot be written in this option list.
.PP
Unmount with
.B umount
.I mountpoint
or
.B fusermount3 \-u
.IR mountpoint .
.SH OPTIONS
Options
.B mount.fuse3
strips before
.B mirrorfs
sees them
.RB ( user ,
.BR users ,
.BR nouser ,
.BR nofail ,
.BR auto ,
.BR noauto ,
.BR _netdev ,
.BR setuid= ,
.BR drop_privileges )
are still accepted when
.B mirrorfs
is invoked directly.
So are
.BR defaults ,
.BR comment ,
.BI comment= text\fR,
and any option whose name starts with
.BR x- .
.TP
.BI remote= path
Remote directory.
Required.
The path must already be a directory, and it must not be the local directory or the mount point.
A folder inside an NFS, SMB, or Ceph mount is accepted, as is the mount root.
.TP
.BI remote\-fs= type
Kernel filesystem type
.I path
must be on.
The name is what
.B findmnt \-n \-o FSTYPE
prints, such as
.BR nfs4 ,
.BR cifs ,
or
.BR ceph .
.B nfs
also matches
.BR nfs4 .
Several types can be joined with
.B :
or by repeating the option.
While the path is a different filesystem, mirrorfs queues mutations and does
not write through that path.
.TP
.BI remote\-check= duration
How often to look for
.BR remote\-fs .
.BR 30s ,
.BR 5m ,
.BR 1h ,
or a number of seconds.
The default is
.BR 5m .
The first look is immediate.
A match opens a directory fd on that mount and drains the queue through it.
Requires
.BR remote\-fs .
.TP
.B allow_other
Let every user access the filesystem.
As root this does not need
.I user_allow_other
in
.IR /etc/fuse.conf .
The kernel still enforces
.BR default_permissions ,
which
.B mirrorfs
always sets.
.TP
.B allow_root
Let root access the filesystem in addition to the mounter.
Ignored when
.B allow_other
is also set.
.TP
.B debug
Append one line per operation to the daemon log.
.TP
.B foreground
Stay in the foreground and log to the terminal.
For a manual run.
Do not use it in
.IR /etc/fstab .
.TP
.B ro
Mount read-only.
The kernel rejects writes, so nothing new is queued.
.TP
.BR rw ", " exec ", " noexec ", " suid ", " nosuid ", " dev ", " nodev
.TQ
.BR atime ", " noatime ", " relatime ", " strictatime ", " lazytime
.TQ
.BR sync ", " async ", " dirsync
Passed through to the FUSE mount.
.B nodev
and
.B nosuid
belong on a normal fstab line.
.B mount.fuse3
adds
.B dev
and
.B suid
when those two were not requested.
.TP
.B default_permissions
Accepted and ignored.
.B mirrorfs
always enables it.
.TP
.B auto_unmount
Rejected.
The daemon outlives the
.B mount
command, so this option would not mean what it means for a foreground FUSE process.
.PP
Any other option is an error and the mount does not proceed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/mirrorfs
Installed filesystem daemon.
.TP
.I /var/log/mirrorfs.log
Log written by a daemonized mount, mode 0644, created on first use.
If it cannot be opened, messages are discarded.
The foreground command logs to the terminal instead.
.TP
.I /etc/fstab
Mount table read by
.BR mount (8)
and by systemd.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mirrorfs (1),
.BR mount (8),
.BR mount.fuse3 (8),
.BR fusermount3 (1),
.BR fstab (5),
.BR systemd.mount (5)
